# Gotchas

## `db.create_all()` and real migrations must never coexist

`core/db.py`'s `init_db()` only calls `db.create_all()` when
`testing=True`:

```python
def init_db(app: Flask, *, testing: bool = False) -> None:
    ...
    db.init_app(app)
    migrate.init_app(app, db)
    if testing:
        # Tests run against an isolated in-memory database with no
        # migration history to replay, so the schema is created directly
        # from the models here instead. Real usage must NOT do this: it
        # would leave `flask db migrate` permanently unable to detect a
        # schema change, since the tables would already match the models
        # before any migration ever ran. Real schema comes from `flask
        # db upgrade` — see README.md's "Migrations" section.
        with app.app_context():
            db.create_all()
```

That `if testing:` guard is load-bearing, not incidental. If
`db.create_all()` ran unconditionally — e.g. moved outside the guard "to
make sure the tables exist" — real usage would create every table
directly from the current models, outside migration history entirely.
The tables would then already exist and already match the models
exactly, so the next `flask db migrate` has nothing to diff: it
permanently reports **"No changes in schema detected"**, even for a
real model change, because as far as Alembic's autogenerate can tell,
the live database already matches. There's no clean way to recover from
this except dropping the tables (losing data) or hand-writing the
migration Alembic refused to generate.

The `testing=True` path is safe specifically because it targets the
isolated in-memory SQLite database (`tests/conftest.py`'s
`sqlite:///:memory:`), which has no migration history and is thrown
away after the test run — never the real, `.env`-configured database.
Real usage establishes schema exclusively through `flask db upgrade`
(see `guides/first-migration.md`). If you're ever tempted to add a
`db.create_all()` call to `main.py`'s startup path "to make first-run
easier," don't — that's precisely the mistake this guard exists to
prevent.

## `flask db migrate` needs a live app, not just an import

Unlike bare Alembic (where `env.py` typically imports a `Base`/engine
directly), this project's `migrations/env.py` pulls both the engine and
the model metadata off `current_app.extensions['migrate'].db` at
runtime — the actual object `migrate.init_app(app, db)` registered. That
means every `flask db` command needs `--app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py`
so Flask can build a real `create_app()` instance first; running
`alembic` directly (skipping the `flask` CLI) in this project won't
work, because there's no app context for `env.py` to pull `current_app`
from.

## Running `flask db init` here fails, and isn't needed

The `flask/rest-api` template ships `migrations/` fully pre-generated —
`env.py`, `alembic.ini`, `script.py.mako`, and an empty `versions/`
directory are already in the generated project. Running `flask db init`
against it fails (Flask-Migrate refuses to overwrite an existing
`migrations/` directory), and it isn't part of this project's workflow
regardless — go straight to `flask db migrate`.
